"Explains a situated experience through a short sequence of visual frames showing actors, context, actions and consequences.\n\nUse when: Communicate a proposed service concept before building it. Discuss how a solution fits a person's real situation.\n\nAvoid when: Detailed rule logic or a complete operational procedure is required.\n\nVisual grammar: Ordered frames represent meaningful moments in a scenario. Simple characters and contextual cues show who acts and where. Brief captions clarify intention or consequence without replacing the visuals.\n\nBuild: Choose one actor, situation and concept to communicate. Select a few moments that establish the problem, interaction and result. Sketch each moment and review whether the scenario is understandable unaided.\n\nCheck: The same actor and context remain coherent across frames. Each frame adds a meaningful event or change. Proposed behavior is distinguished from observed research evidence.\n\nPitfalls: Filling panels with interface screenshots and losing the surrounding situation. Depicting an implausibly effortless experience that hides critical assumptions.\n\nExample: A cyclist notices a fault, requests a mobile repair, meets the technician and resumes the journey, showing the service in context.\n\nAlternatives: Customer journey map, Test card."
